Run 2 — fixed 25% checkpoint analysis, version 1.2.2

Use RUN_PHASE_25_ANALYSIS.command --workers 10. Leave the continuous downloader
running. Wait for CHECKPOINT_025_SAVED, not just the terminal percentage passing
25. The analysis reads the immutable phase_results/25 snapshot and never reads
or changes the downloader's live SQLite journals or latest-phase pointer.

Expected runtime: roughly 1–3 hours for the first 25% run, informed by the actual
74-minute 10% run. This is not a 25% laptop benchmark. New games still require an
all-history chronology scan; Parquet indexes and completed features are reused.
Resume skips completed stages. Offline tests normally take 20–90 seconds.
The terminal prints stage progress, elapsed times and approximate stage ETAs.

What runs
1. Original frozen ordinary-game placebo: later granting and reaching a choice.
2. Original frozen entry equations: all five outcomes, with the designated
   analysis_entry_90 outcome and within-cohort two-primary-test Holm correction.
3. Cumulative new 25%, the new 10–25% increment, the original 10% hash band,
   the old author-clean comparison, and their pooled sample. The increment
   contains distinct games but can share accounts and later outcomes with 10%.
4. Exact game/feature bridge to the completed 10% analysis, ignoring only
   reassigned row IDs; numerical reproduction of its coefficients and SEs.
5. SECONDARY ordinary-game checks using competitive-only historical scores
   (disconnected player's cp >= -100; at least five past decisions), against
   the pooled score on identical rows under X0 and XC controls. Both use the
   same author-clean cross-fitted state reference, strict lag and pair removal.
6. SECONDARY upper-tail sensitivity: drop scores above the 95th percentile
   of raw competitive scores in supported new-phase25 ordinary rows. Apply
   that cutoff to all cohorts, keep cutoff ties, and refit the controls. This
   changes instrument support and does not establish instrument validity.

The primary specifications, original fixed score SD, endings, calendar rules,
prospective spacing stream, account identities, and shared-account OR date CR0
covariance stay fixed. No new matching, outcome search, or new chronological
scan is needed for the secondary scores. Main receipt IV estimates are not rerun.
The competitive secondary uses the previous Oct 3 S1/XC SD = 0.09545502195393069
as a declared unit only; that author-included reference unit does not make this
an author-clean receipt re-estimation. The pooled unit remains 0.059411690344000595.

Interim interpretation
All 25% intervals and equivalence checks are nominal interim descriptions.
Repeated phases are not independent replications. The 10–25% band was specified
before viewing its outcomes, but this is a post-result diagnostic program.
The nominal +/-0.015 pp TOST margin and original +0.020385913 pp/SD sensitivity
benchmark remain separately reported. No abstract/title or causal-identification
gate is automatically cleared, and download continuation never depends on results.

Authors __PRIVATE_VALUE_1__ and __PRIVATE_VALUE_2__ are removed from focal analytical games, behavioral
histories, state-reference fitting and later choices. Factual chronology and
full exposure spacing streams are preserved. Two focal records are explicitly quarantined: the original April 2024 cache
conflict and the June 2025 saved-response flag in the new 10–25% band. Their
identities, phase membership and absence from retained pools are checked.
The immutable c7362f8697b5 coverage and its exact counts bind this amendment.
Record-level evidence stays on the Mac; the report contains field/error diagnostics.
Any other unresolved metadata stops. No replacement or imputation is made.

Performance
Narrow Parquet projection, immutable exact-game feature blocks, reusable donor,
recipient and future-choice indexes, early eligibility screening, bounded-sort
chronology and up to 10 spawned workers. At an 11 GiB budget: six feature workers,
one dense regression at a time. Each worker has one numerical/Arrow/DuckDB
thread. This avoids oversubscribing RAM. No API requests, Stockfish or rating replay.
Cache definitions for existing features are byte-for-byte compatible with v1.1.0.
Only missing games are computed. The 25% sample never substitutes a cache-selected
sample for the frozen full-SHA256 hash threshold.

Paths
Project: /Volumes/XT_Pro/lichess_kindness
Desktop: ~/Desktop/Lichess_Desktop
Python: /Volumes/XT_Pro/lichess_kindness/venv/bin/python
Overrides: IV_PROJECT_ROOT, IV_DESKTOP, IV_PYTHON; CLI --workers/--memory-gb/--plan.
Analysis: derived/replication/run2_phase25_analysis_v120_PRIVATE/<run>/
Shared cache: derived/replication/run2_feature_cache_v1_PRIVATE/
Report: RUN2_PHASE_025_ANALYSIS_REPORT_<run>.zip and .zip.sha256 on Desktop.
Final status: PHASE_025_AVAILABLE_CASE_ANALYSIS_COMPLETE.
A PHASE_025_PRIMARY_COMPLETE_SECONDARY_PENDING report already contains primary fits.
Return the small aggregate ZIP and checksum, not individual game/cache files.

This package is validated with synthetic inputs here. The production Parquets
remain on your Mac; no new 25% empirical result has been computed in ChatGPT.

Packaging correction (v1.2.1)
v1.2.0 omitted two nested PACKAGE_MANIFEST.sha256 files from its ZIP. This
release includes both original, unchanged manifests, and adds a regression
test that verifies nested-manifest closure from the extracted package.
The launcher is tested from a fresh extraction of the final ZIP.
Analysis specifications, data sources and feature-cache definitions are unchanged.

Two-record continuation (v1.2.2)
The reviewed 25% coverage has 14,814,253 fixed additional-frame rows;
7,931,804 retained pool games and 5,160,774 ordinary games before chronology.
There are two unresolved focal records, one each in April 2024 and June 2025.
The old one-record runner intentionally stopped. This is an explicit interim
available-case amendment made before seeing any 25% model estimates.
Both records remain unresolved and in the fixed denominator. Private audit
Parquets preserve their identities and source values. The June error codes
and field comparisons are exported in metadata_quarantine.json.
Original April identity is checked against the previous completed audit.
June must be the flagged saved response in the exact 10–25% hash band.
Counts are reported as one excluded focal record in each disjoint band, two
in cumulative new_phase25/pooled, and zero in old_author_clean. Factual history
and prospective spacing streams are unchanged. No influence bound is asserted.

Completed v1.2.1 coverage Parquets are authenticated and reused by hard link
(or copied across file systems), without rerunning the metadata joins.
Expect roughly 2–5 minutes for authentication and the two-record audit, then
roughly 1–3 hours for the full first analysis; these are laptop estimates.
The source inputs and feature-cache definitions remain compatible with v1.1.0.
